---
import DocsNav from "../../components/DocsNav.astro";
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import { docs, repositoryUrl } from "../../data/site";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Architecture | OrbitKV"
  description="The complete pinned SGLang and OrbitKV native RuntimeSession product boundary."
  active="Architecture"
>
  <header class="page-hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">Architecture</p>
    <h1>One product. One KV authority.</h1>
    <p>
      OrbitKV combines the complete pinned SGLang source with one native Rust
      RuntimeSession. The compiler turns retention semantics into checked plans;
      RuntimeSession owns KV lifecycle and physical-page selection on every
      admitted route.
    </p>
  </header>

  <div class="docs-layout">
    <DocsNav active="Architecture" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>Single-product pipeline</h2>
      <pre><code>{`Attention and retention semantics
    -> canonical RuntimeManifest
    -> pinned SGLang RuntimeTarget admission
    -> exact RuntimeBinding
    -> wire 14 / 48 exports / 78 layouts
    -> native RuntimeSession
    -> SGLang tensor and kernel effects
    -> completion evidence
    -> publication and reclamation ACK`}</code></pre>

      <h2>Complete product boundary</h2>
      <p>
        The shipped source product preserves the complete pinned SGLang engine
        and applies the reviewed OrbitKV integration inside that tree. It is not
        a reduced fork, a sidecar manager, or a second engine. SGLang continues
        to own serving, scheduling, tensor allocation, model execution, kernels,
        and stream/event creation. One native <code>RuntimeSession</code> owns its
        accepted retention plan, logical identities, physical-page selection,
        Prefix and copy-on-write decisions, semantic and execution frontiers,
        retirement, acknowledgement, and safe reuse.
      </p>
      <p>
        The old raw manager ABI, optional structured data plane, neutral
        reference product, and second-engine path have been deleted. Python
        retains only the engine identities, checked mirrors, tickets, and
        completion evidence needed to connect SGLang effects to the native
        session; it is not a parallel lifecycle authority.
      </p>

      <h2>Closed typed boundary</h2>
      <p>
        The live boundary is <code>WIRE_VERSION = 14</code>, with exactly
        <strong>48 exported symbols</strong> and <strong>78 checked layouts</strong>.
        The native library, C declarations, Python loader, RuntimeTarget, and
        RuntimeBinding must agree. A version, symbol-set, layout, target, or
        binding mismatch fails closed before allocation or execution.
      </p>

      <h2>Compiler artifacts</h2>
      <p>
        <code>RuntimeManifest</code> is the canonical fingerprinted compiler
        artifact. Its declarative source lowers into derived plans and capability
        requirements. <code>RuntimeTarget</code> describes the pinned SGLang
        admission boundary, and <code>RuntimeBinding</code> proves that one exact
        manifest was admitted by one exact target. These are generic product
        contracts: hardware, checkpoint, and evidence-directory names belong to
        qualification provenance, not to architecture terminology.
      </p>

      <h2>Ownership boundary</h2>
      <table class="evidence-table-native">
        <thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Authority</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Compiler artifacts</td><td>Declarative semantics, derived physical plans, capabilities, fingerprints, and static admission</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Native RuntimeSession</td><td>Request and snapshot identity, physical-page selection, generations, Prefix/COW decisions, frontiers, retirement, ACK, and reuse</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Pinned SGLang</td><td>Serving, scheduling, tensor allocation, kernels, model execution, and stream/event creation</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h2>Safety invariants</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Logical identity is separate from physical slot and generation.</li>
        <li>Immutable snapshot heads are authoritative; engine mirrors are checked effects.</li>
        <li>Unsupported semantics and engine contracts fail closed before mutation.</li>
        <li>Ambiguous post-mutation outcomes fail stop instead of guessing ownership.</li>
        <li>Reuse requires semantic death, execution completion, and exact acknowledgement.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Native RuntimeSession profiles</h2>
      <p>
        Five constrained profiles have host coverage on the sole native route:
        whole-domain Full token state, ordered Full+Sliding token state, pure
        Sliding token state, exact Chunked token state, and request-private Full
        latent state. Sharing and Prefix behavior remain profile-specific; no
        profile inherits another profile's support or evidence. Layouts such as
        rings are compiler-derived physical plans, not separate products or the
        architectural contribution.
      </p>
      <p>
        Full+Sliding and pure Sliding both still need live wire-14 real-device
        correctness and performance qualification. Other profiles retain their
        separately documented host and evidence boundaries.
      </p>

      <h2>Semantic and execution frontiers</h2>
      <p>
        Token-level reclamation requires two independent proofs. The Semantic
        Frontier shows that a logical token is no longer reachable from live
        snapshots. The Execution Frontier shows that prior device work can no
        longer read or write its storage. RuntimeSession permits reuse only after
        both proofs and the exact SGLang acknowledgement are present. Relocation
        is byte-exact state movement and reclamation, not numerical compression
        or an automatic capacity benefit.
      </p>

      <h2>Qualification boundary</h2>
      <p>
        Historical Full accelerator records are useful earlier-wire correctness
        evidence for their captured source, workload, and environment. They
        reported no speedup, and they do not qualify the live wire-14 product.
        Evidence provenance may name the exact device, checkpoint, source
        revision, or record location; those names do not become generic
        architecture labels. Host, real-device correctness, performance,
        capacity, and production qualification remain separate claims.
      </p>
      <p>
        The <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/docs/capability-matrix.md`}>Capability Matrix</a>{" "}
        is normative. Detailed provenance and archived observations live in the{" "}
        <a href={`${repositoryUrl}/blob/main/results/README.md`}>Results Index</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Product documents</h2>
      <div class="doc-grid">
        {docs.map((item) => (
          <div class="doc-card">
            <span>{item.key}</span>
            <h3>{item.name}</h3>
            <p>{item.detail}</p>
            <a href={item.href}>Open document →</a>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
